          "english_quote": "it will be more bearable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah on the day of judgment than to persist in the place of disobedience",
          "issue": "The draft gives the comparison uneven grammatical scope: the first dative becomes the party for whom something is bearable, while the second becomes the location of an infinitive. The final ἐνδιατελεῖν can instead supply the subject of the impersonal comparison and govern both parallel locative datives. The existing note discusses only the second dative and does not disclose this materially different attachment. The English consequently leaves unclear what experiences are being compared.",
          "suggested_revision": "A source-transparent candidate is: “it will be more bearable to remain in the land of Sodom and Gomorrah on the day of judgment than in the place of disobedience.” Explain that this takes the final infinitive across both coordinated datives. Preserve the alternative attachment of γῇ to “bearable for” in a note, acknowledging the uneven comparison it produces rather than presenting the present mixed construction without explanation. The amended rendering requires fresh review.",
